WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:05.460
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:06.780
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.060
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:09.320
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.120
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:20.120
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.280
We're the Office Ladies.

8
00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:26.180
Morning, lady.

9
00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:29.740
Oh, we have a really amazing guest on Office Ladies today.

10
00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:31.960
We sure do.

11
00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:38.820
All of you that listened to Work Bus last week know that today in the studio, we have Bryan Cranston.

12
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:46.980
We cannot wait for you guys to hear this interview because, look, there are certain episodes of The Office that are just real big episodes.

13
00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.880
And they warrant extra time.

14
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.480
And Work Bus is definitely one of those.

15
00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:52.840
It's definitely one of those.

16
00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:53.520
Yeah.

17
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.740
And we shared all about our experience on that bus.

18
00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:00.040
But we really felt like you needed to hear from the director as well.

19
00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:02.000
So we reached out to Bryan.

20
00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.260
He was so kind and gracious.

21
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.340
He came into the studio and chatted all things Work Bus with us.

22
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:09.200
And more.

23
00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.440
Now, I know a lot of people know Bryan Cranston from his many acting roles.

24
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.440
He's been in film and television and he's been on Broadway.

25
00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.980
You know, there's Malcolm in the Middle and Walter White on Breaking Bad.

26
00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:24.340
And he even won a Tony Award for the Broadway play All the Way.

27
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:25.860
He's amazing.

28
00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:29.040
But Bryan is also a very accomplished director.

29
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:37.920
He directed episodes of Malcolm in the Middle, of Breaking Bad, Modern Family, Sneaky Pete, and his new series, Your Honor, on Showtime.

30
00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:43.160
And, of course, this very epic, famous episode of The Office.

31
00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:45.760
And he is just delightful.

32
00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:48.000
He's so much fun to talk to.

33
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.760
It was such a joy having him.

34
00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:52.180
And Jenna, you know what?

35
00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:58.320
I was thinking we didn't bring this up to him, but I want him to direct our road trip movie if we ever do it.

36
00:01:58.560 --> 00:01:59.860
Oh, my gosh.

37
00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:00.500
Yes.

38
00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:01.300
Yes.

39
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.500
We've never even shared.

40
00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:06.240
Have we shared about the fact that me, you, and Lee have a road trip idea?

41
00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.000
We came up with an idea for a road trip movie.

42
00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:11.400
Lee wrote it.

43
00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.140
It's so funny.

44
00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:14.560
It's so funny.

45
00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:15.420
I know.

46
00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:21.960
Some of the hardest I have laughed in a long time was when the three of us were sitting at your dining table and we were pitching jokes.

47
00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:25.340
Because, listen, you guys, this is a road trip movie about two gals.

48
00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.320
They're, like, in their 50s.

49
00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:28.760
Yes.

50
00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:30.280
But anyway, I mean.

51
00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.620
It has a mom detective's element to it, I have to say.

52
00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:34.820
It really does.

53
00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:35.480
Yeah.

54
00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:39.460
But would Bryan Cranston ever direct anything on the road again?

55
00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:40.520
That's the question.

56
00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:46.780
I think after you listen to this interview, you might find that he would be very hesitant to do so.

57
00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:54.500
Well, listen, the office might have ruined him forever in directing anything in a moving vehicle again.

58
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.980
But let's let him tell you about it.

59
00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:58.420
But we're going to take a break.

60
00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:05.440
And when we come back, our interview with Bryan Cranston about Work Bus, which is also known as Death Bus.

61
00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:06.540
We'll be right back.

62
00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:07.400
It's such a great interview.

63
00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:08.420
We'll be right back.

64
00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:20.700
Yeah.

65
00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:21.600
Yeah.

66
00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.140
Well, who wrote your opening theme song?

67
00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:26.320
Creed Bratton.

68
00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:26.980
Did he really?

69
00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:27.280
Yes.

70
00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:30.380
You know, I just realized who Creed was.

71
00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:31.880
You just did.

72
00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:35.720
I just in the last few weeks.

73
00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:37.200
Grassroots, all of that.

74
00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:37.580
Yes.

75
00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:38.000
Yeah.

76
00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:38.580
The grassroots.

77
00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.060
And I'm thinking, that can't be the same.

78
00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:42.140
It's the same guy.

79
00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:42.480
Yes.

80
00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:45.140
Now, my question to you, my first question.

81
00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:46.460
Thanks for coming on the show, by the way.

82
00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:56.540
My first question to you would be, did the show ever do a backstage idea about what Creed's background was?

83
00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:59.640
Did someone ever go, that guy looks so familiar?

84
00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:03.080
And, you know, did you ever do anything like that?

85
00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:03.880
I don't remember.

86
00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:05.340
There were so many pitches.

87
00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:15.160
There were so many people who wanted to do a whole episode just on Creed and his life and wanted him to be outed as Creed Bratton.

88
00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:21.280
Creed Bratton, because he's the only person on the show who has his full name as his character name.

89
00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:25.840
Like, Angela is Angela on the show, but on the show her last name is Martin.

90
00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:26.300
Right.

91
00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:29.540
But Creed Bratton, the character, is also Creed Bratton, the man.

92
00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.580
But I don't think it was ever revealed.

93
00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.140
I will say, though, of all the characters, we have a show Bible.

94
00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:35.760
Sorry.

95
00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:36.840
I have to.

96
00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:37.600
You guys.

97
00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:38.380
Brian.

98
00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:40.220
Brian.

99
00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.900
His microphone's this morning.

100
00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:43.760
Something's up with it.

101
00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.740
And there is a bar in front of his face.

102
00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:48.680
And so for me to talk to Brian, he has to look lean around.

103
00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:50.820
Angela, it's safer to have a bar in front of my face.

104
00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:55.100
It helps the restraining order issue that we have.

105
00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:55.700
That we have.

106
00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:01.840
But anyway, of all the characters, we had a show Bible of all their back history.

107
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.200
And Creed's is the crazieSt.

108
00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:05.780
It's the most fun to read.

109
00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:08.020
He has like a family in Canada.

110
00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:10.280
He lives under his desk half the time.

111
00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.240
It's like all over the place.

112
00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:12.420
Wait a minute.

113
00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.640
Why didn't I get a chance to read that?

114
00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:16.200
The show Bible.

115
00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:17.440
They didn't give you the show Bible?

116
00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:18.460
No, I didn't get it.

117
00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:20.360
No one said it was available.

118
00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:21.500
Been given that.

119
00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:23.920
I absolutely should have been given that.

120
00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:25.060
Would have changed Workbus.

121
00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:25.340
I'm going to quit.

122
00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:25.780
Yeah.

123
00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:28.360
Retroactive.

124
00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:34.080
Well, we always like to ask people when they come on, first question, how did you end up

125
00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:34.820
working on The Office?

126
00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:35.400
Oh.

127
00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.560
How'd you get this job directing Workbus?

128
00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:40.200
Tracy Katsky is a friend of mine.

129
00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:47.580
And she was working as a consultant with David in the last year you guys were on the air.

130
00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:48.060
Okay.

131
00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:48.360
Right?

132
00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:56.860
And I had just finished my season with Breaking Bad, and that was 2012.

133
00:05:57.280 --> 00:05:57.500
Yep.

134
00:05:57.760 --> 00:05:58.160
I think.

135
00:05:58.440 --> 00:05:59.620
It was 2012.

136
00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:05.220
And Tracy calls and says, we're looking for directors for this new season.

137
00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:08.380
Would you be interested in coming on board The Office?

138
00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:11.100
And immediately the answer was going to be, of course, no.

139
00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:15.740
But I love the show.

140
00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:21.780
It's such a legendary show that any chance to be a part of it in some small way.

141
00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:30.820
And she said, I know you've been directing Breaking Bad and, you know, you're out in the desert and it's hot and it's sticky.

142
00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:37.060
How would you like to come to, what was it, Burbank, North Hollywood, wherever we were?

143
00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:39.980
And we work inside.

144
00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:40.460
Yeah.

145
00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:41.300
A little off comedy.

146
00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:41.940
It's a short day.

147
00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:42.720
Air conditioned.

148
00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:43.480
Air conditioned.

149
00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:44.360
Easy.

150
00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:44.780
Yeah.

151
00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:45.480
Indoors.

152
00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:46.260
Five days.

153
00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:47.220
Bang, bang, bang.

154
00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:47.840
Boom, boom, boom.

155
00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.260
And I said, oh, it sounds fantastic.

156
00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:51.880
Let's do it.

157
00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:54.980
And she said, well, we have such and such slot open.

158
00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.780
And I looked at my calendar and I said, I'll take that slot.

159
00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:05.860
And I don't know, you probably described it on your show before, but the way it works in television for directors is by time.

160
00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:13.680
You have no idea when you sign on to a show what your episode is going to be by the time it gets to your time slot.

161
00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:15.500
So we had no idea.

162
00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:21.840
And it just so happened that work.

163
00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:25.640
Work bus came on to my lap.

164
00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:29.060
What did you think when you read this episode?

165
00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:33.760
Well, the first, I was excited to read it, read it right away.

166
00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:36.100
And work bus, oh, I don't know.

167
00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:38.800
Oh, they exterminate.

168
00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:39.820
Was it exterminating?

169
00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:41.280
No, we were exterminating.

170
00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:42.340
This is 11 years ago, I think.

171
00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:47.140
There was this electrical issue, electromagnetic things in the walls.

172
00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:49.780
And Dwight was going to have to open up all the walls and redo the electrical.

173
00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:59.040
So the basic idea is that there could not be a work stoppage for the whole team.

174
00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:11.020
So a work bus was found to be the best method to continue working while this work on the actual location.

175
00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:12.560
The office was taking place.

176
00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:14.940
So everybody had to load onto the bus.

177
00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:17.420
Desks were on there, as we all know.

178
00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:21.100
And we had to just keep traveling.

179
00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:24.520
We're in the San Fernando Valley in AuguSt.

180
00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:26.340
It's so hot in AuguSt.

181
00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:26.660
It's so hot.

182
00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:28.240
I went to the call sheets.

183
00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:30.160
It was 100 degrees this week.

184
00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:31.260
It was like triple digits.

185
00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:32.540
It was very hot.

186
00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:40.300
So the first thing I did was curse my friend Tracy Katzky and say, damn you, as if they knew, you know.

187
00:08:40.540 --> 00:08:42.800
But it was a challenge.

188
00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:48.360
I think we shot in on the set for a day and a half or something like that.

189
00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:48.880
Yeah, yeah.

190
00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:49.180
Something short.

191
00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:52.540
And then the rest of the time we were on a bus.

192
00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:54.120
On that bus or in the park.

193
00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:55.880
And then we were at a baseball field.

194
00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:56.600
I know we were.

195
00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:56.960
Yep, right.

196
00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:58.540
We had that cold open.

197
00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:02.800
I enjoyed it for the most part.

198
00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:06.860
There were some tough moments.

199
00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:13.320
Well, I have to say, okay, you know that our cast has renamed this episode Death Bus.

200
00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:14.860
This has come to you.

201
00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:15.280
I know.

202
00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:20.580
And Jenna and I wrote a whole chapter in our book about it, The Office BFFs.

203
00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:22.160
We have a copy for you.

204
00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:22.600
We signed.

205
00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:23.220
Oh, I love it.

206
00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:25.640
Inside it says, Brian, we owe you our lives.

207
00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:27.560
Thanks, Angela and Jenna.

208
00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:29.980
It is chapter 12.

209
00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:35.320
And we dedicated a whole chapter to just this episode.

210
00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:38.220
And we have fantastic photos of you.

211
00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:42.820
But we realized after our book came out, you were doing press.

212
00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:49.640
We felt really bad because every single producer clearly wanted you to be asked about Death Bus.

213
00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:51.520
Jenna was like, oh, no, Ange.

214
00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:53.160
Poor Brian.

215
00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:58.320
We wrote about this, and now everyone is asking him the same question on every talk show.

216
00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:00.640
Why did you try to kill the cast of The Office?

217
00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:02.080
Why did I try to kill the cast?

218
00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:04.480
Was it a murder for hire?

219
00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:05.560
No one's asked.

220
00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:08.100
Was it a failed murder for it?

221
00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:09.920
I can't discuss all those details.

222
00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:10.210
Right.

223
00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:14.480
There is a current investigation going on.

224
00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:14.860
Ongoing.

225
00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:15.560
Ongoing.

226
00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:19.020
And, you know, indictments will be handed down.

227
00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:23.640
I have every reason to believe you two are in the clear.

228
00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:26.000
I can't be positive on that.

229
00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:29.540
Set of circumstances.

230
00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:32.560
It was hot and tight.

231
00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:36.740
You know, you talk about the set being contained.

232
00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:38.840
Well, we're on a bus.

233
00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:39.420
Yeah.

234
00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:41.180
And we didn't have a lot of room.

235
00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:46.660
And if I recall, nothing was actually nailed down.

236
00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:49.240
Everything was kind of shifting around.

237
00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:49.580
Correct.

238
00:10:49.780 --> 00:10:51.920
Desks were actually shifting and moving.

239
00:10:51.920 --> 00:11:04.660
And I'm looking at this going, oh, my God, I think people didn't realize this bus is actually on the road, on an open street, and moving.

240
00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:07.720
Well, at first, there was comedy in that, right?

241
00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:12.800
Like, at first, we're traveling down the road, and, like, our little coffee cup is moving across the desk.

242
00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:13.660
Our papers.

243
00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:15.580
We're holding on to things.

244
00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:17.420
We hit a pothole.

245
00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:18.860
My lamp slid in my lap.

246
00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:19.620
I caught it.

247
00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:20.120
Yay.

248
00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:21.040
That's funny.

249
00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:21.560
It's funny.

250
00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:30.700
But then there was a part of the script where, I guess, Dwight swerves, and we all kind of slide, and we all are like, ah.

251
00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:32.760
Yeah, he's trying to get us to the pie.

252
00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:33.900
Yeah, get to the pie.

253
00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:39.100
And I guess the AD yelled to the cast, we weren't really swerving.

254
00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:40.720
Lean right.

255
00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:41.920
And we kind of, ah.

256
00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:43.260
And no one bought it.

257
00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:44.240
It didn't look good.

258
00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:46.680
And so then they unloaded Rain.

259
