00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:22.180
Rolling down.

521
00:22:22.180 --> 00:22:22.420
Kumbaya.

522
00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:24.700
Shibuya roll call.

523
00:22:25.220 --> 00:22:26.420
The big roll call.

524
00:22:28.680 --> 00:22:33.980
Well, Leslie, I remember looking at Leslie David Baker, and his eyes were bloodshot and watering.

525
00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:37.340
And I kept thinking, like, maybe the vent is hitting him at an angle.

526
00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:39.940
You know how it dries out your eyes, like if a vent hits you?

527
00:22:40.140 --> 00:22:42.060
But no, it was just poison.

528
00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:44.780
Now, was Leslie's desk close to yours?

529
00:22:44.920 --> 00:22:45.260
Yes.

530
00:22:45.260 --> 00:22:46.060
I'm trying to remember.

531
00:22:46.240 --> 00:22:53.580
When we had the, when we all went flying into one another, I believe I flew into Leslie.

532
00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:58.280
Did Leslie, when we got off, I remember he was like, I'm not getting back on.

533
00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:00.780
Leslie said, I'm never getting back on the bus again.

534
00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:01.300
I'm done.

535
00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:02.320
I'm done getting on the bus.

536
00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:06.640
I remember him walking to his car and driving away, but I think that might not be true.

537
00:23:06.820 --> 00:23:09.700
But for some reason, I remember Leslie was like, goodbye.

538
00:23:10.160 --> 00:23:10.700
I'm out.

539
00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:11.280
I'm out.

540
00:23:11.480 --> 00:23:12.100
Shoot around me.

541
00:23:12.100 --> 00:23:16.480
But also, poor Leslie, whenever we went on locations, he got the worst of it.

542
00:23:16.580 --> 00:23:21.980
When we went to the beach, he got sand kicked in his eye, and it scratched his cornea, and

543
00:23:21.980 --> 00:23:24.120
he had to wear, like, a patch for a week.

544
00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:24.360
Yeah.

545
00:23:24.760 --> 00:23:25.080
Oof.

546
00:23:25.460 --> 00:23:28.980
So, I think Leslie was like, I don't do scenes outside of the office anymore.

547
00:23:29.140 --> 00:23:29.640
Forget it.

548
00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:30.380
Forget it.

549
00:23:30.720 --> 00:23:32.200
I don't go on location.

550
00:23:32.340 --> 00:23:33.240
Yeah, shit goes down.

551
00:23:33.240 --> 00:23:34.940
It almost sounds like his character.

552
00:23:35.220 --> 00:23:36.040
You just drill.

553
00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:37.800
I don't go outside.

554
00:23:38.140 --> 00:23:38.820
Yeah, exactly.

555
00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:39.780
Don't leave the office.

556
00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:45.260
Were there any moments that you haven't talked about about this episode?

557
00:23:45.380 --> 00:23:49.900
Because we know you've been hit up with those questions quite a bit, but any behind-the-scenes

558
00:23:49.900 --> 00:23:52.020
memories that you have that you'd want to share?

559
00:23:52.340 --> 00:23:52.700
Yeah.

560
00:23:52.900 --> 00:23:59.780
There was—those things are so on the frontal lobe that it was—it's hard to remember.

561
00:23:59.980 --> 00:24:05.320
It was just—I just had a really good time other than worrying about that.

562
00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:14.760
I was really happy to be able to be on your set and see it and feel it, and you guys—by

563
00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:22.000
the time I came along, it was the ninth season, so you were a well-oiled machine, and the role

564
00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:29.420
of a guest director is not to come in and—I wouldn't dare, wouldn't dare say anything

565
00:24:29.420 --> 00:24:34.700
to an actor about their character, how—wouldn't that be obnoxious?

566
00:24:34.900 --> 00:24:36.280
Like, season nine.

567
00:24:36.400 --> 00:24:38.700
I think you could make a different choice for Dwight.

568
00:24:38.940 --> 00:24:41.540
I think—let's make this—I think he's friendlier.

569
00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:42.040
Yeah.

570
00:24:43.520 --> 00:24:45.120
Why is Angela such a bitch?

571
00:24:45.340 --> 00:24:45.620
Yeah.

572
00:24:46.660 --> 00:24:47.600
What's with the cats?

573
00:24:47.740 --> 00:24:49.020
Let's do away with—let's forget all that.

574
00:24:49.020 --> 00:24:49.460
No more cats.

575
00:24:49.580 --> 00:24:50.060
No more cats.

576
00:24:50.060 --> 00:24:54.520
Um, yeah, and then you two, you know, I'd say, I'd pull you apart.

577
00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:55.240
Yeah.

578
00:24:55.240 --> 00:24:57.760
Start to think about something about Jim that you didn't like.

579
00:24:58.200 --> 00:24:58.700
Yeah.

580
00:24:59.240 --> 00:25:01.280
Just throw a wrench into everything.

581
00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:07.440
He's like, I don't think this—so there's nothing—a director on episodic television just

582
00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:15.440
comes in and tries to—what my whole goal was, whenever I direct television, which is different

583
00:25:15.440 --> 00:25:20.640
from directing features, because in features, it's—it's—you—you are trying and need

584
00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:26.100
to—to please yourself and—and make sure that you get all those elements and moments

585
00:25:26.100 --> 00:25:29.120
that you've worked so hard in breaking down that script.

586
00:25:29.380 --> 00:25:36.120
In television, it—it's about staying true to these characters, putting them in positions

587
00:25:36.120 --> 00:25:39.060
where they can really be themselves, where they feel comfortable.

588
00:25:39.500 --> 00:25:45.240
It's delivering the meat and potatoes that every showrunner is going to expect from you.

589
00:25:45.440 --> 00:25:45.760
Mm-hmm.

590
00:25:45.880 --> 00:25:54.960
I always thought, if I can deliver two or three things that the showrunner didn't see, that's—that's

591
00:25:54.960 --> 00:25:55.380
golden.

592
00:25:55.680 --> 00:25:58.280
Just a couple little—couple little things.

593
00:25:58.580 --> 00:25:59.740
I think there's a few.

594
00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:05.660
I mean, when I rewatched the episode, I completely forgot that Ed plays the banjo at the end.

595
00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:06.580
Oh, yeah, yeah.

596
00:26:06.580 --> 00:26:08.840
He—I mean, and just rips it.

597
00:26:08.980 --> 00:26:12.140
It's just so great, and everyone is just like, oh, whatever.

598
00:26:12.340 --> 00:26:14.520
Well, no, it's—it's—and it's true.

599
00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:20.500
I taught him the banjo in two weeks, and he was able to pick it up and perform that.

600
00:26:20.520 --> 00:26:20.940
That's amazing.

601
00:26:21.120 --> 00:26:21.280
Yeah.

602
00:26:21.580 --> 00:26:23.260
I loved all the pie scenes, too.

603
00:26:23.380 --> 00:26:24.440
I thought they were really sweet.

604
00:26:24.820 --> 00:26:25.820
Did you eat any pie?

605
00:26:25.900 --> 00:26:26.620
I had some pie.

606
00:26:26.620 --> 00:26:28.520
Do you remember your flavor of pie?

607
00:26:28.700 --> 00:26:30.260
That's probably hard to remember.

608
00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:31.980
I'm thinking gooseberry?

609
00:26:32.560 --> 00:26:33.040
Yeah.

610
00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:33.900
Yeah.

611
00:26:34.140 --> 00:26:36.340
That was—look how happy you were.

612
00:26:36.340 --> 00:26:37.520
Well, he remembered that.

613
00:26:37.620 --> 00:26:38.540
Wasn't that a film?

614
00:26:38.900 --> 00:26:39.200
Yeah.

615
00:26:39.620 --> 00:26:41.100
Because it's such a funny name.

616
00:26:41.260 --> 00:26:42.060
I like it, and I like it.

617
00:26:42.060 --> 00:26:47.300
Yeah, there was like a—it was like a—I think it was gooseberry, because it wasn't blackberry.

618
00:26:47.480 --> 00:26:47.740
No.

619
00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:50.720
It was—yeah, but there was a special berry crumble pie.

620
00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:51.340
Pie.

621
00:26:51.520 --> 00:26:51.580
Pie.

622
00:26:51.580 --> 00:26:56.220
When I rewatched it, I just remember how happy we actually all were to sit outside and have

623
00:26:56.220 --> 00:26:56.620
pie.

624
00:26:57.400 --> 00:26:57.740
Yeah.

625
00:26:57.960 --> 00:26:58.920
I had chocolate cream.

626
00:26:59.740 --> 00:26:59.980
Mm-hmm.

627
00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:00.900
It was delicious.

628
00:27:00.900 --> 00:27:02.660
Interesting choice on a very hot day.

629
00:27:02.960 --> 00:27:03.020
Huh?

630
00:27:03.320 --> 00:27:03.580
What?

631
00:27:03.860 --> 00:27:04.760
Chocolate cream.

632
00:27:05.200 --> 00:27:05.360
What?

633
00:27:05.680 --> 00:27:06.860
Peel back the layers.

634
00:27:17.630 --> 00:27:18.070
Okay.

635
00:27:18.070 --> 00:27:19.490
I have got to do something.

636
00:27:19.710 --> 00:27:23.990
I have been going through the call sheets, and on the back of the call sheet, they do

637
00:27:23.990 --> 00:27:29.190
a thing called Get to Know Your Cast and Crew, and they ask a few questions, but they're

638
00:27:29.190 --> 00:27:30.270
so interesting.

639
00:27:30.610 --> 00:27:34.210
You mean on every one of your call sheets, it was Get to Know Your Cast and Crew?

640
00:27:34.630 --> 00:27:36.810
There—someone was featured on each call sheet.

641
00:27:36.810 --> 00:27:42.630
We had talked about—Jen and I wanted to start with each new guest, ask them a few of these

642
00:27:42.630 --> 00:27:43.110
questions.

643
00:27:43.310 --> 00:27:46.530
What was one of the first jobs you had in entertainment?

644
00:27:47.450 --> 00:27:53.110
One of the first jobs I had in entertainment, I think it was a soap opera.

645
00:27:53.450 --> 00:27:58.070
I was—I did like a three-day role on Days of Our Lives.

646
00:27:58.130 --> 00:28:00.070
I love Days of Our Lives!

647
00:28:00.590 --> 00:28:03.370
Back in 1981?

648
00:28:04.630 --> 00:28:06.090
What was your character?

649
00:28:06.090 --> 00:28:09.130
I was a cousin of the person.

650
00:28:09.450 --> 00:28:10.630
I will tell you this.

651
00:28:10.890 --> 00:28:16.830
Bill and Susan Seaforth, he and his wife, Susan and Bill, they were on Days of Our Lives

652
00:28:16.830 --> 00:28:17.370
for years.

653
00:28:17.790 --> 00:28:24.410
I happened to oversleep that morning the first day that I was to work, and I missed the

654
00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:27.070
rehearsal, missed the blocking, missed everything.

655
00:28:27.230 --> 00:28:28.570
I'm panicking.

656
00:28:28.890 --> 00:28:29.150
Yeah.

657
00:28:29.230 --> 00:28:30.490
Absolutely panicking.

658
00:28:30.490 --> 00:28:35.770
And I race to NBC where we're shooting it, and I'm panicking.

659
00:28:35.770 --> 00:28:36.490
I'm nervous.

660
00:28:36.490 --> 00:28:41.430
And all of a sudden, I hear a knocking on my dressing room door, and it's Bill Hayes,

661
00:28:41.490 --> 00:28:42.790
the big star of the show.

662
00:28:42.910 --> 00:28:43.090
Yeah.

663
00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:48.870
And he says, I understand you had a problem this morning, and we stood in for you, and

664
00:28:48.870 --> 00:28:54.190
we blocked it, and he says, can I run down what we did, and you need some help on the

665
00:28:54.970 --> 00:28:55.990
backstory of the character?

666
00:28:56.210 --> 00:28:57.930
I was so appreciative.

667
00:28:57.930 --> 00:29:02.790
I was just a day player, and he put himself out there to say, look.

668
00:29:02.850 --> 00:29:03.330
That is so kind.

669
00:29:03.470 --> 00:29:03.650
Yeah.

670
00:29:03.810 --> 00:29:11.810
He was a lovely, lovely guy, and he helped me get over the anxiety of being late and the

671
00:29:11.810 --> 00:29:13.250
responsibility of that.

672
00:29:13.350 --> 00:29:20.490
And I spent all the money that I made on that episode to buy flowers for the people that

673
00:29:20.490 --> 00:29:26.070
I knew stood up for me, and Bill and the casting director, and it was like, I'm so sorry.

674
00:29:26.070 --> 00:29:28.730
But that was my, yeah, it was my first experience.

675
00:29:29.430 --> 00:29:29.690
Okay.

676
00:29:29.910 --> 00:29:30.650
I love that.

677
00:29:30.710 --> 00:29:31.390
I love that story.

678
00:29:31.390 --> 00:29:32.690
I love the kindness you were shown.

679
00:29:32.930 --> 00:29:35.350
I didn't know where that was going when you said he knocked on the door.

680
00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:36.110
I was like, oh, no.

681
00:29:36.230 --> 00:29:36.610
Yeah, no.

682
00:29:37.010 --> 00:29:37.750
In a good way.

683
00:29:37.970 --> 00:29:38.530
In a good way.

684
00:29:38.930 --> 00:29:39.270
Okay.

685
00:29:40.070 --> 00:29:43.150
Favorite place in the whole wide world that you've ever been to?

686
00:29:43.390 --> 00:29:44.930
I guess it would be Venice.

687
00:29:45.310 --> 00:29:51.250
I was there on my honeymoon almost 35 years ago.

688
00:29:51.450 --> 00:29:53.590
Very romantic and very unique.

689
00:29:53.590 --> 00:30:02.350
And we just had, there was one night when my wife and I were, there was a thunderous rainstorm.

690
00:30:02.630 --> 00:30:11.130
And we went to a Vivaldi concert in a church that had windows at the top of this sphere

691
00:30:11.130 --> 00:30:13.390
and this dome.

692
00:30:13.390 --> 00:30:19.310
And it felt like God was adding the exclamation to it.

693
00:30:19.550 --> 00:30:20.630
Da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da!

694
00:30:20.670 --> 00:30:21.110
Bleh!

695
00:30:21.650 --> 00:30:23.550
You know, and we're like, oh, my God.

696
00:30:23.830 --> 00:30:28.190
It was just so, it was just so magical and romantic.

697
00:30:28.570 --> 00:30:29.450
Unbelievable time.

698
00:30:29.770 --> 00:30:30.190
Wow.

699
00:30:30.330 --> 00:30:32.150
Have you been back since your honeymoon?

700
00:30:32.670 --> 00:30:33.450
Actually, no.

701
00:30:33.550 --> 00:30:34.570
It's been a long time.

702
00:30:35.190 --> 00:30:36.310
I've been there twice.

703
00:30:36.510 --> 00:30:38.590
We haven't been back in 34 years.

704
00:30:38.750 --> 00:30:39.610
It's time to go back.

705
00:30:39.610 --> 00:30:40.490
I think it's time.

706
00:30:40.730 --> 00:30:40.970
Yeah.

707
00:30:41.550 --> 00:30:42.190
I don't know.

708
00:30:42.370 --> 00:30:43.670
I say don't mess with it.

709
00:30:44.070 --> 00:30:44.510
What?

710
00:30:45.030 --> 00:30:46.290
I say leave it.

711
00:30:46.450 --> 00:30:47.250
Don't revisit.

712
00:30:47.550 --> 00:30:48.710
Never revisit a memory.

713
00:30:48.890 --> 00:30:50.110
Well, it's too good.

714
00:30:50.230 --> 00:30:51.290
It's too good.

715
00:30:51.450 --> 00:30:52.330
You know what I mean?

716
00:30:52.330 --> 00:30:56.650
If you go back and then you're going to be like, oh, my gosh, it smells.

717
00:30:57.010 --> 00:30:57.410
What?

718
00:30:57.850 --> 00:31:02.330
I mean, no offense, Venice, but I hear that you're getting floods and maybe it's a little

719
00:31:02.330 --> 00:31:07.470
stinky now and it's a lot of tourists because the cruise ships and the-

720
00:31:07.470 --> 00:31:08.410
All the Venetians.

721
00:31:10.150 --> 00:31:11.270
I've lost the whole-

722
00:31:11.270 --> 00:31:13.650
The amount of gondoliers that listen to the show.

723
00:31:13.830 --> 00:31:14.870
It's just incredible.

724
00:31:14.870 --> 00:31:16.030
We just lost a few.

725
00:31:16.450 --> 00:31:21.430
Well, I'm just saying, you know, a lot of time has passed and this is an amazing romantic

726
00:31:21.430 --> 00:31:21.950
memory.

727
00:31:22.150 --> 00:31:24.250
Maybe it should just stay there.

728
00:31:24.250 --> 00:31:25.450
I agree with you.

729
00:31:25.450 --> 00:31:25.790
Stay there.

730
00:31:25.910 --> 00:31:26.610
I agree with you.

731
00:31:26.870 --> 00:31:31.610
Maybe in the church and the concert and the rain and all that, but I don't think you need

732
00:31:31.610 --> 00:31:33.250
to rule out all of Venice.

733
00:31:33.470 --> 00:31:36.390
I think there's maybe new places you could discover.

734
00:31:36.550 --> 00:31:36.790
Yeah.

735
00:31:37.030 --> 00:31:38.010
I think leave it.

736
00:31:38.290 --> 00:31:38.670
Okay.

737
00:31:38.790 --> 00:31:40.130
Well, I'm torn now.

738
00:31:40.230 --> 00:31:43.030
Two very different takes on this memory.

739
00:31:43.510 --> 00:31:43.690
Okay.

740
00:31:43.830 --> 00:31:44.570
Last question.

741
00:31:44.930 --> 00:31:45.950
They just asked three.

742
00:31:46.630 --> 00:31:47.230
Last question.

743
00:31:47.430 --> 00:31:50.530
Do you speak any other languages other than English?

744
00:31:50.890 --> 00:31:51.050
Oui.

745
00:31:51.550 --> 00:31:51.830
Oui.

746
00:31:52.250 --> 00:31:52.770
Ho, ho.

747
00:31:52.770 --> 00:31:55.550
I don't, actually.

748
00:31:57.770 --> 00:31:59.490
That was the extent of my French.

749
00:31:59.630 --> 00:31:59.750
No.

750
00:31:59.950 --> 00:32:04.350
I know a little bit of French and a little bit of Spanish, but that's one of the things

751
00:32:04.350 --> 00:32:11.370
I really, really do want to accomplish before I leave this earth is to be able to expand

752
00:32:11.370 --> 00:32:18.930
my awareness and culture and not just be so American-centric.

753
00:32:19.230 --> 00:32:20.730
I don't want to step out of that.

754
00:32:20.730 --> 00:32:21.690
Yeah.

755
00:32:21.970 --> 00:32:26.590
I spoke recently about not retiring, but taking a break.

756
00:32:26.970 --> 00:32:30.410
For the last 25 years, I've been working really nonstop.

757
00:32:30.750 --> 00:32:30.830
Yeah.

758
00:32:30.830 --> 00:32:31.550
And it's been great.

759
00:32:31.550 --> 00:32:38.110
As we know, it's wonderful, and you're supported, and you're having fun, and you're engaged, and

760
00:32:38.110 --> 00:32:40.970
it's artistic and creative, and all those wonderful things.

761
00:32:41.090 --> 00:32:43.270
But it's not real-life experience.

762
00:32:43.650 --> 00:32:44.010
Yeah.

763
00:32:44.010 --> 00:32:46.490
You're going from one bubble to another bubble to another bubble.

764
00:32:46.490 --> 00:32:53.330
And I'm getting to a point where I feel like I'm a little depleted, like my ideas aren't

765
00:32:53.330 --> 00:33:00.590
as fresh, they're a little harder to come by, and ooh, am I being derivative of another

766
00:33:00.590 --> 00:33:03.510
character that I've done, and can I break out of that?

767
00:33:03.510 --> 00:33:09.650
I need more influences to come in so that it's more reciprocal.

768
00:33:10.090 --> 00:33:16.010
Right now, there's been a lot of outflow for the last two decades, and I think I need some

769
00:33:16.010 --> 00:33:22.050
real-life experience to be able to say, oh, I've never even thought of that before.

770
00:33:22.050 --> 00:33:27.070
Or I want to read that classic novel that I've always sworn I was going to read.

771
00:33:27.270 --> 00:33:29.010
I want to learn how to cook.

772
00:33:29.230 --> 00:33:31.270
I want to learn a language.

773
00:33:31.630 --> 00:33:35.830
I want to be in another culture, and I don't want to talk about show business.

774
00:33:36.430 --> 00:33:36.630
Yeah.

775
