1280
00:55:30.850 --> 00:55:34.990
And it's done, Dos Hombres is now the fifth largest mezcal in the world.

1281
00:55:35.130 --> 00:55:35.950
Come on.

1282
00:55:35.950 --> 00:55:37.730
That is so amazing.

1283
00:55:38.330 --> 00:55:39.670
I have, we have to try it.

1284
00:55:39.710 --> 00:55:40.610
We have to try it.

1285
00:55:40.650 --> 00:55:41.270
We have to try it.

1286
00:55:41.350 --> 00:55:42.210
We're going to go get some.

1287
00:55:42.450 --> 00:55:43.770
I will bring it to you.

1288
00:55:43.890 --> 00:55:44.210
Yay.

1289
00:55:44.510 --> 00:55:45.370
I will bring it to you.

1290
00:55:45.370 --> 00:55:45.910
Even better.

1291
00:55:46.070 --> 00:55:47.150
I love this.

1292
00:55:47.350 --> 00:55:47.530
Yeah.

1293
00:55:47.530 --> 00:55:48.070
That's really good.

1294
00:55:48.070 --> 00:55:49.550
Just your description of it.

1295
00:55:49.670 --> 00:55:50.750
Just, I don't know.

1296
00:55:50.830 --> 00:55:52.710
I felt like I left my body for a minute.

1297
00:55:52.870 --> 00:55:54.830
It's felt like I went on a little vacation.

1298
00:55:55.030 --> 00:55:55.530
I know.

1299
00:55:56.030 --> 00:55:56.810
A journey.

1300
00:55:57.030 --> 00:55:57.510
And we did.

1301
00:55:57.670 --> 00:55:58.570
We went down to Oaxaca.

1302
00:55:58.730 --> 00:56:03.470
We found this spirit and brought it to the marketplace.

1303
00:56:04.350 --> 00:56:11.370
And in truth, the spirit found us because it's been around for hundreds of years and we were just there to receive it.

1304
00:56:11.370 --> 00:56:16.230
This sounds like a pretty good start on the project of living life.

1305
00:56:16.750 --> 00:56:17.650
It is.

1306
00:56:17.930 --> 00:56:22.930
But like any other business, it takes a lot of effort and a lot of time and energy.

1307
00:56:23.150 --> 00:56:25.870
You're probably more than you expected when you started.

1308
00:56:25.870 --> 00:56:42.050
And it's a lot of work and I find that the business end of it, the case cells and this, it's not as interesting to me as the process of making it and that nurturing sense.

1309
00:56:42.350 --> 00:56:47.190
Yeah, that and I have a production company and that's interesting too and it's good.

1310
00:56:47.190 --> 00:56:57.190
But I'm now looking for ways to have more of that human, real life experience.

1311
00:56:57.530 --> 00:56:59.710
I have a crazy question for you.

1312
00:56:59.890 --> 00:57:00.210
Oh.

1313
00:57:00.770 --> 00:57:06.810
Did you ever in your young artist life ever do that book, The Artist's Way?

1314
00:57:07.210 --> 00:57:07.330
No.

1315
00:57:07.330 --> 00:57:08.250
By Julia Cameron?

1316
00:57:08.610 --> 00:57:08.790
No.

1317
00:57:09.430 --> 00:57:10.010
Excellent.

1318
00:57:10.350 --> 00:57:11.710
I highly recommend it.

1319
00:57:11.910 --> 00:57:13.030
I highly recommend it.

1320
00:57:13.210 --> 00:57:14.110
Jenna did it twice.

1321
00:57:14.110 --> 00:57:17.850
I did a few pages of it.

1322
00:57:18.490 --> 00:57:20.170
And how did the few pages go?

1323
00:57:20.970 --> 00:57:22.150
We're fantastic.

1324
00:57:22.850 --> 00:57:24.070
We're different personalities.

1325
00:57:25.810 --> 00:57:36.590
I did it twice because the first time I didn't finish and it bothered me so deeply because I realized that one of my challenges in my early artistic life was having great,

1326
00:57:36.830 --> 00:57:39.550
tons of great ideas of which I finished none.

1327
00:57:39.710 --> 00:57:40.170
Right.

1328
00:57:40.170 --> 00:57:44.710
And so I thought, I need to get over this finishing things problem.

1329
00:57:44.850 --> 00:57:44.970
Right.

1330
00:57:45.450 --> 00:57:49.110
Why don't I start by finishing The Artist's Way?

1331
00:57:49.270 --> 00:57:49.570
Yes.

1332
00:57:49.730 --> 00:57:53.990
And so I had done 10 of the 12 weeks and I went back and I started over again.

1333
00:57:54.150 --> 00:57:55.490
I did all 12 weeks.

1334
00:57:55.610 --> 00:57:59.810
And there are still things from that book, even today as an artist, that I think of.

1335
00:57:59.810 --> 00:58:06.050
You know, one of the things is once a week you have to go on an artist's date alone by yourself, just you.

1336
00:58:06.410 --> 00:58:09.330
That could be going to a symphony.

1337
00:58:09.490 --> 00:58:11.630
That could be sitting in a park and watching people.

1338
00:58:11.810 --> 00:58:15.590
That could be taking yourself bowling alone.

1339
00:58:15.830 --> 00:58:20.070
Like whatever your artist's date is, you have to do this once a week.

1340
00:58:20.110 --> 00:58:21.550
But it's all creative.

1341
00:58:21.550 --> 00:58:22.410
It's all artistic.

1342
00:58:22.410 --> 00:58:31.070
And so sometimes when I'm like starting a new artistic journey or whatever, I'll refer back and I'll do an artist's date and I'll try to kickstart something.

1343
00:58:31.330 --> 00:58:33.510
It's that business of living life, right?

1344
00:58:34.350 --> 00:58:35.310
That's fantastic.

1345
00:58:35.670 --> 00:58:36.670
It's really awesome.

1346
00:58:36.770 --> 00:58:38.030
The bowling part threw me.

1347
00:58:38.190 --> 00:58:38.550
The bowling.

1348
00:58:38.630 --> 00:58:40.510
That's an artist's date if you want it to be.

1349
00:58:40.690 --> 00:58:40.910
Really?

1350
00:58:40.910 --> 00:58:41.490
Oh, yeah.

1351
00:58:41.750 --> 00:58:42.670
Take yourself bowling.

1352
00:58:42.930 --> 00:58:44.630
To be fair, the week I got the book.

1353
00:58:44.630 --> 00:58:45.370
Go to an art museum.

1354
00:58:45.650 --> 00:58:50.490
It was the week of my birthday when I got this book as a gift, but I also got a pair of roller skates.

1355
00:58:50.950 --> 00:58:53.610
And I was a child of the 70s and I loved roller skating.

1356
00:58:53.830 --> 00:58:57.210
And then I think I started it and then I was like, I'm going to go roller skate.

1357
00:58:57.690 --> 00:58:58.830
That could be an artist's date.

1358
00:58:59.170 --> 00:59:01.470
Well, it was an artist's date that never stopped.

1359
00:59:01.490 --> 00:59:04.050
It was an artist's date that never – I still have the same pair of roller skates.

1360
00:59:04.190 --> 00:59:05.010
They're by my front door.

1361
00:59:05.750 --> 00:59:06.890
I still have roller skates.

1362
00:59:06.990 --> 00:59:08.410
That's got to be something, Jenna.

1363
00:59:08.630 --> 00:59:09.190
That's great.

1364
00:59:09.390 --> 00:59:10.770
That came out of the artist's way for me.

1365
00:59:10.910 --> 00:59:12.510
I think it came out of your birthday.

1366
00:59:12.690 --> 00:59:14.330
My birthday buddy is Ricky Gervais.

1367
00:59:15.210 --> 00:59:16.470
Oh, look at that.

1368
00:59:16.750 --> 00:59:17.870
A little tie-in.

1369
00:59:18.630 --> 00:59:20.130
I once told him that.

1370
00:59:20.390 --> 00:59:21.310
He was like, okay.

1371
00:59:22.350 --> 00:59:24.490
We also have Rachel Weisz.

1372
00:59:25.710 --> 00:59:27.710
Oh, does she share our birthday as well?

1373
00:59:27.850 --> 00:59:27.950
Yes.

1374
00:59:29.970 --> 00:59:30.770
Peter Sarsgaard.

1375
00:59:31.810 --> 00:59:32.770
This is exciting.

1376
00:59:32.870 --> 00:59:33.750
We should have a dinner.

1377
00:59:34.250 --> 00:59:35.390
We should have a March 7th.

1378
00:59:35.510 --> 00:59:36.630
Busy Phillips has my birthday.

1379
00:59:36.790 --> 00:59:37.410
Busy Phillips, yeah.

1380
00:59:37.510 --> 00:59:39.830
You, Busy Phillips, Ricky Gervais are going to have dinner.

1381
00:59:39.830 --> 00:59:41.170
Oh, my God.

1382
00:59:41.310 --> 00:59:42.090
I would love that dinner.

1383
00:59:42.430 --> 00:59:42.830
And then we'll have dinner with Rachel.

1384
00:59:43.530 --> 00:59:45.350
That's not a bad idea.

1385
00:59:45.530 --> 00:59:46.230
Birthday dinner?

1386
00:59:46.230 --> 00:59:47.050
For a show.

1387
00:59:47.310 --> 00:59:47.650
Oh.

1388
00:59:47.890 --> 00:59:52.850
You have a dinner with all the people who share the date and you find the similarities

1389
00:59:52.850 --> 00:59:56.210
and the differences between them, but they all have the same birthday.

1390
00:59:56.410 --> 00:59:58.130
Brian, you have a production company.

1391
00:59:58.290 --> 00:59:58.810
I'm so sorry.

1392
00:59:58.810 --> 01:00:00.230
We're putting you to work again.

1393
01:00:00.590 --> 01:00:02.050
This has to stop.

1394
01:00:02.050 --> 01:00:05.530
Rather than that, let me give it to you and I take 10%.

1395
01:00:05.530 --> 01:00:06.890
There it is.

1396
01:00:07.270 --> 01:00:07.710
That's it.

1397
01:00:07.870 --> 01:00:08.010
Perfect.

1398
01:00:08.190 --> 01:00:09.030
And welcome to Hollywood.

1399
01:00:11.330 --> 01:00:13.510
Thank you so much for coming in today.

1400
01:00:13.870 --> 01:00:13.920
This was fun.

1401
01:00:13.920 --> 01:00:14.250
Thank you.

1402
01:00:14.390 --> 01:00:16.210
It was so good to see you again.

1403
01:00:16.390 --> 01:00:16.670
You too.

1404
01:00:16.870 --> 01:00:18.790
You know, this episode, it was beautiful.

1405
01:00:19.050 --> 01:00:19.730
It turned out great.

1406
01:00:19.730 --> 01:00:22.590
I just rewatched it and it's so good.

1407
01:00:22.610 --> 01:00:27.490
Even though we all almost died, it's a great episode and it's a classic and people love

1408
01:00:27.490 --> 01:00:28.230
it and they don't know.

1409
01:00:28.450 --> 01:00:32.690
It is a classic and now when people see us fly across the bus, I think we needed that

1410
01:00:32.690 --> 01:00:32.970
moment.

1411
01:00:33.070 --> 01:00:33.450
We did.

1412
01:00:33.590 --> 01:00:34.310
I think we needed it.

1413
01:00:34.310 --> 01:00:35.830
I think it made the pie that much sweeter.

1414
01:00:36.010 --> 01:00:36.870
We did use it.

1415
01:00:36.970 --> 01:00:37.410
I noticed.

1416
01:00:37.410 --> 01:00:38.390
It's the actual tape.

1417
01:00:38.390 --> 01:00:38.830
It's in there.

1418
01:00:39.110 --> 01:00:39.270
Yeah.

1419
01:00:39.570 --> 01:00:41.710
And now you can relive it in our book.

1420
01:00:41.730 --> 01:00:43.230
Thank you, Angela.

1421
01:00:43.430 --> 01:00:44.290
That's so cool.

1422
01:00:44.890 --> 01:00:45.250
Yeah.

1423
01:00:45.870 --> 01:00:47.350
Oh, thank you very much.

1424
01:00:47.510 --> 01:00:47.690
You're welcome.

1425
01:00:47.750 --> 01:00:48.690
We love you so much.

1426
01:00:48.690 --> 01:00:52.370
I'm going to bring you some Dos Hombres and we'll all sip some.

1427
01:00:52.370 --> 01:00:53.770
Do you have it on ice?

1428
01:00:53.970 --> 01:00:55.190
Do you have it straight?

1429
01:00:55.470 --> 01:00:57.530
Yes, I...

1430
01:00:57.530 --> 01:00:58.510
How do you have it?

1431
01:00:58.690 --> 01:01:02.770
You know, any way you like it is the right way.

1432
01:01:02.950 --> 01:01:03.170
Okay.

1433
01:01:03.230 --> 01:01:04.950
I like it just neat.

1434
01:01:05.870 --> 01:01:11.750
Maybe one big ice cube and just let it chill without diluting it and just sip it.

1435
01:01:11.850 --> 01:01:14.310
It's so great just sipping it.

1436
01:01:14.990 --> 01:01:16.290
Do you ever mix it?

1437
01:01:16.410 --> 01:01:16.830
Oh, yeah.

1438
01:01:17.070 --> 01:01:17.790
All the time.

1439
01:01:17.830 --> 01:01:18.990
It goes in a cocktail as well.

1440
01:01:19.130 --> 01:01:24.510
It goes so well in a margarita, in a Paloma, in an old-fashioned Negroni.

1441
01:01:24.510 --> 01:01:27.190
I mean, it just goes on and on and on.

1442
01:01:27.190 --> 01:01:27.510
Oh, yeah.

1443
01:01:27.770 --> 01:01:28.430
Those are my faves.

1444
01:01:28.570 --> 01:01:29.050
So good.

1445
01:01:29.370 --> 01:01:29.590
Yeah.

1446
01:01:29.670 --> 01:01:30.970
Try a Mezcal Negroni.

1447
01:01:31.070 --> 01:01:31.870
Ooh, that sounds good.

1448
01:01:31.870 --> 01:01:33.070
You're going to love it.

1449
01:01:37.160 --> 01:01:38.460
Well, there you have it.

1450
01:01:38.460 --> 01:01:40.940
Our interview with Bryan Cranston.

1451
01:01:41.360 --> 01:01:43.480
I could not have enjoyed that more, Angela.

1452
01:01:43.700 --> 01:01:44.540
He's the beSt.

1453
01:01:44.740 --> 01:01:45.380
The beSt.

1454
01:01:45.520 --> 01:01:45.700
Yeah.

1455
01:01:46.140 --> 01:01:50.660
Big thank you to Bryan for joining us in the studio, being so gracious with his time.

1456
